# Decomposition

**Decomposition** is the splitting of a complex system or problem into its
constituent components or subproblems. It is the inverse of [[synthesis]]. The
discussion names it in one table row and does not discuss it, but it uses the
operation repeatedly, and those uses are the best account of it here.

## Description

| | Input | Output |
|---|---|---|
| Decomposition | complex system or problem | constituent components or subproblems |

Two different things get decomposed.

- **A task.** A problem is cut into subproblems that can be handled one at a
  time. This is the sense in the table of operations for language models, and
  it is how an agent turns a request into steps.
- **A concept.** A notion that was treated as one thing is shown to be
  several. The discussion's main corrections have this form.

Examples of the second kind, from the discussion:

- "Self-reference" is split into eight classes, from recursive generation to
  self-modeling cognition, with the remark that they overlap and are not all
  strange loops. That is the [[taxonomy-of-self-reference]].
- "Irreducible" is split into three notions that do not imply one another:
  algorithmic incompressibility, [[computational-irreducibility]] and
  [[undecidability]].
- "Induction" is split into inference from evidence and a deductive proof
  scheme.
- "Strange loop" itself is split into seven candidate definitions.

In each case the decomposition did the work a definition could not. Asked for
one formal definition, the discussion answered with a taxonomy instead.

Decomposing a task and decomposing a recursive computation look alike, and
differ. [[recursion]] cuts a problem into smaller instances of the same
problem. Decomposition in general cuts it into parts of different kinds.

## Relations

- It is one [[cognitive-operation]] {relation: instance-of}: a complex whole in, components out.
- Its inverse is [[synthesis]] {relation: contrasts}.
- [[taxonomy-of-self-reference]] {relation: example-of} decomposes one notion into eight classes.
- It differs from [[recursion]] {relation: distinguished-from}: parts of different kinds, against smaller instances of the same kind.
- It differs from [[abstraction]] {relation: distinguished-from}: abstraction drops detail to find one structure, decomposition keeps everything and separates it.
- It differs from [[simplification]] {relation: distinguished-from}: nothing is approximated away.
- It bears on the question [[learning-the-formalism]] {relation: answers}, as one of the operations mapped there.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[synthesis]].** Combining several structures into one. It is what a
  decomposition has to be followed by if the whole is to be recovered.
- **[[taxonomy-of-self-reference]].** Eight classes of self-reference with
  their mechanisms. It is the discussion's largest decomposition of a concept.
- **[[recursion]].** Definition through smaller instances of the same thing.
  It is the special case in which every part resembles the whole.

